## Purpose

When having one outer pattern X with two similar inner patterns Y, their CSS definitions can affect each other.

### Example
```
> Experience E
  > Pattern X
    > Pattern Y
      > Container
        - cfBackgroundColor is blue on desktop by default (`defaultValue` inside Pattern Y)
        - cfBackgroundColor is green on mobile (override inside Pattern X)
    > Pattern Y
      > Container
        - cfBackgroundColor is blue on desktop by default (`defaultValue` inside Pattern Y)
```

Both render **blue** on all breakpoints as defined by default on Pattern Y.
Inside Pattern X, the mobile color for the first inner pattern is set to **green**.

Until now, both inner pattern nodes would use the same CSS class name for desktop as the name is generated via `breakpointId` and a hash of the generated CSS code (which is equal for desktop). Another class name is generated for the mobile styles of the first inner pattern and only added to that one. As a result, the DOM will contain the following styles:
```
<styles>
.cfstyles-nodeDesktopHash {
  background-color: blue;
}
@media (max-width 512px) {
  .cfstyles-nodeMobileHash {
    background-color: green;
  }
}
</style>
<styles>
.cfstyles-nodeDesktopHash {
  background-color: blue;
}
</style>
```

⚡️⚡️⚡️ As a result, both container nodes will be rendered **blue** on mobile.

💡 This is because **media queries have no effect on specificity** and thus, the styles for the least added node are applied to all nodes with the same class name.

## Approach

To fix this, we have to ensure that nodes with the same ID (i.e. they are instances of the same pattern) do not overlap.

- With this PR, the optional prefix to the node class name is added during SSR generation
- In CompositionBlock we use the same logic to achieve uniqueness

